   "evidence": "The proof body is `rw [intervalIntegral_mul_exp_comp_exp (fun y \u21a6 max (G y) 0), mul_comm L U, mul_comm \u03b7 U, Real.exp_mul, Real.exp_mul]`, which uses no property of `max`. Roadmap \u00a76.9(b) gives the substitution identity only for `G\u207a`, which is one instance of the general statement.",
   "file": "TauCeti/Analysis/MellinTransform/Windows.lean",
   "fix": "State it for an arbitrary `g : \u211d \u2192 \u211d`: `(\u2203\u1da0 Y in atTop, (\u222b y in Y..(Y ^ L), g y * y ^ (-(\u03b8 + 1))) \u2264 Y ^ \u03b7) \u2194 \u2203\u1da0 U in atTop, (\u222b u in U..(L * U), g (Real.exp u) * Real.exp (-\u03b8 * u)) \u2264 Real.exp (\u03b7 * U)`. In `HasWindows.frequently_integral_mul_rpow_le_rpow`, instantiate it with `g := fun y \u21a6 max (G y) 0`.",
   "issue": "`frequently_integral_mul_rpow_le_rpow_iff` puts `max (G y) 0` into both sides of the statement. The proof only rewrites with `intervalIntegral_mul_exp_comp_exp (fun y \u21a6 max (G y) 0)` and never uses nonnegativity, so the equivalence holds for every `g : \u211d \u2192 \u211d`. As written, it cannot be applied to other integrands, such as `|G|` or the signed integrand.",
   "line": 32
  }
 ],
